1
00:04:14,285 --> 00:04:20,285
A picture as sensational as its subject!

2
00:08:28,571 --> 00:08:34,571
Five Star Final is a crime, drama film directed by Mervyn LeRoy.

3
00:12:42,857 --> 00:12:48,857
Searching for headlines at any cost, an unscrupulous newspaper owner forces his editor
to print a serial based on a past murder, tormenting a woman involved.

4
00:16:57,142 --> 00:17:03,142
Joseph W. Randall, played by Edward G. Robinson.

5
00:21:11,428 --> 00:21:17,428
Jenny Townsend, played by Marian Marsh.

6
00:25:25,714 --> 00:25:31,714
Michael Townsend, played by H.B. Warner.

7
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:46,000
Phillip Weeks, played by Anthony Bushell.

8
00:33:54,285 --> 00:34:00,285
Ziggie Feinstein, played by George E. Stone.

9
00:38:08,571 --> 00:38:14,571
Nancy (Voorhees) Townsend, played by Frances Starr.

10
00:42:22,857 --> 00:42:28,857
Five Star Final is a 1931 American pre-Code drama film about the excesses of
tabloid journalism directed by Mervyn LeRoy and starring Edward G.

11
00:46:37,142 --> 00:46:43,142
Robinson, Aline MacMahon (in her screen debut) and Boris Karloff.

12
00:50:51,428 --> 00:50:57,428
The screenplay was by Robert Lord and Byron Morgan based on the
1930 play of the same name by Louis Weitzenkorn.

13
00:55:05,714 --> 00:55:11,714
The title refers to the practice of newspapers publishing a series of editions throughout the day, with their final-edition front page having five stars
printed and the word "Final." "Five Star Final" is also a font introduced during World War I and then favored by newspapers for its narrow type.

14
00:59:20,000 --> 00:59:26,000
Warner Bros.

15
01:03:34,285 --> 01:03:40,285
remade the film in 1936 as Two Against the World, also known as One Fatal Hour, starring Humphrey
Bogart in Robinson's part and set in a radio station instead of at a newspaper.

16
01:07:48,571 --> 01:07:54,571
The film was nominated at the 5th Academy Awards (1931/1932)
for Best Picture, but lost to Grand Hotel.

17
01:12:02,857 --> 01:12:08,857
Newspaper publisher William Randolph Hearst was greatly offended by the film, which
he interpreted as a thinly veiled attack on him and his operation.

18
01:16:17,142 --> 01:16:23,142
He retaliated by publishing negative reviews in his papers
and pressuring theaters not to show the film.

19
01:20:31,428 --> 01:20:37,428
The film explores themes of press, based on play or musical,
newspaper man, newspaper editor, pre-code.

20
01:24:45,714 --> 01:24:51,714
The tone is emotional.
